  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Friday)\nIssued at 215 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nMost guidance forecasts weak upper level ridging to develop \nbriefly over portions of central Alabama and much of Georgia \nMonday night in response to an upper low aloft moving through \neastern Texas into NW Lousiana. Some weak westerly to slightly \nsouthwesterly low level flow will likely bring a bit more moisture\nin the area. This will likely help to limit further freeze \npotential. Cloud cover remains much closer and just east of the \nupper low. Thus, clear skies and light winds should allow for some\nfrost to occur still, but should not be as widespread as previous\nnights. Currently, low temperatures are expected to drop into the\nmid 30s to lower 40s primarily. \n\nDuring the day on Tuesday, models differ on how quickly the upper\nlow pushes east and how much it weakens. Either way though, would\nexpect general warm air advection in low levels to be in place \nahead of it over northern Alabama. Cloud cover also is not very \ncertain this far east. Given expected low level moisture and \ntemperature advection expected, highs should continue to warm \nduring the day into the 65 to 70 degree range in most locations\n(despite any cloud cover).\n\nModels continue to diverge concerning the evolution of the upper\nlow Tuesday afternoon through Wednesday. Expect more cloud cover \nTuesday afternoon or evening and generally steady state temperatures\nduring that period. Either way, the forecast looks precipitation\nfree during that period. \n\nModel guidance does start to converge more towards a stronger \nlongwave trough axis rotating southeast from the Upper Midwest \nlate Wednesday afternoon into Wednesday night. How much this \nphases with a secondary trough axis to its south over Texas is in \nquestion in guidance (which would lend itself to a wetter solution\nand more cloud cover further east later).\n\nA large area of high pressure behind this longwave trough axis is\nshown in most guidance. This originates from north central Canada.\nThis could bring even colder air southward into the area towards\nthe end of the week into the following weekend. This looks like it\ncould be the coldest air of the season so far.",
